{ q: "The contemporary economic importance of French is seen in:", options: ["Decline of luxury sector", "Global market of Francophone countries and luxury/fashion industries", "Only agriculture", "No economy"], ans: "Global market of Francophone countries and luxury/fashion industries", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },
{ q: "French is vital in international arbitration and law because:", options: ["It is never used", "It is a major language of treaties, ICJ, ICC", "English replaced it completely", "Only national law"], ans: "It is a major language of treaties, ICJ, ICC", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },
{ q: "By 2050, projections suggest French speakers could number:", options: ["100 million", "700–800 million", "2 billion", "50 million"], ans: "700–800 million", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },
{ q: "French holds importance in global media through:", options: ["Only local TV", "France 24, TV5Monde, RFI reaching millions worldwide", "No global media", "BBC only"], ans: "France 24, TV5Monde, RFI reaching millions worldwide", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },
{ q: "The language policy of many African Union bodies still recognizes French because:", options: ["Africa is Anglophone", "Strong Francophone bloc and historical usage", "No recognition", "Mandarin dominant"], ans: "Strong Francophone bloc and historical usage", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },
{ q: "French is important in contemporary science and research due to:", options: ["No publications", "Numerous journals and collaborations in Francophone academic networks", "English monopoly", "Only humanities"], ans: "Numerous journals and collaborations in Francophone academic networks", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },
{ q: "Overall, the contemporary global status of French is best described as:", options: ["Dying language", "A major international language with growing speakers in the Global South", "Regional European language only", "Second to Spanish"], ans: "A major international language with growing speakers in the Global South", topic: "Status and importance of French in contemporary world" },

// 20 questions for "Varieties of French in the world today"
{ q: "Which variety of French is characterized by vowel shifts and expressions like 'tuque' for 'beanie'?", options: ["Belgian French", "Québécois French", "Swiss French", "African French"], ans: "Québécois French", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "In African French, the use of 'on va là-bas' to mean 'let's go' is an example of:", options: ["European syntax only", "Influence of local substrate languages", "Standard Parisian usage", "English borrowing"], ans: "Influence of local substrate languages", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Belgian French is distinguished by:", options: ["Use of 'nonante' for 90 and 'septante' for 70", "Identical numbering to France", "Only 'quatre-vingts'", "No differences"], ans: "Use of 'nonante' for 90 and 'septante' for 70", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "The variety spoken in Senegal, Côte d'Ivoire, and Cameroon is often called:", options: ["Maghrebi French", "Français africain / African French", "Antillean Creole", "Standard French"], ans: "Français africain / African French", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Swiss French differs from standard French in:", options: ["Pronunciation of final consonants and numbers like 'huitante'", "No differences", "Only vocabulary", "Complete separation"], ans: "Pronunciation of final consonants and numbers like 'huitante'", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Maghrebi French (North Africa) frequently incorporates words from:", options: ["Wolof and Bambara", "Arabic and Berber", "Creole", "Portuguese"], ans: "Arabic and Berber", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "In Louisiana French (Cajun / Creole), a major influence comes from:", options: ["Modern Parisian", "English and historical Acadian French", "Only Spanish", "No influence"], ans: "English and historical Acadian French", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "The term 'Français de souche' often refers to:", options: ["African varieties", "Traditional metropolitan French", "Québécois only", "Belgian"], ans: "Traditional metropolitan French", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "A common feature of many African varieties is the use of:", options: ["Reduplication and discourse markers from local languages", "Only standard syntax", "No discourse markers", "German influence"], ans: "Reduplication and discourse markers from local languages", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Québécois French preserves some archaic features such as:", options: ["Modern slang only", "Verb conjugations and vocabulary lost in France (e.g., 'charcher' for chercher)", "No archaic features", "Only new words"], ans: "Verb conjugations and vocabulary lost in France (e.g., 'charcher' for chercher)", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "In Haiti, the everyday spoken language is:", options: ["Standard French", "Haitian Creole (with French influence)", "English", "Spanish"], ans: "Haitian Creole (with French influence)", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Cameroonian French is notable for its:", options: ["Absence of local influence", "Code-switching with English and local languages", "Pure Parisian accent", "No code-switching"], ans: "Code-switching with English and local languages", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "The variety spoken in Lebanon and the Maghreb often includes:", options: ["Only French words", "Extensive Arabic loanwords and code-mixing", "No Arabic", "Only slang"], ans: "Extensive Arabic loanwords and code-mixing", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Acadian French (New Brunswick, Nova Scotia) is characterized by:", options: ["Identical to Québécois", "Distinct phonological traits and vocabulary preservation", "Modern French only", "No distinction"], ans: "Distinct phonological traits and vocabulary preservation", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "In contemporary global French, the most rapidly evolving varieties are found in:", options: ["Europe", "Sub-Saharan Africa", "Canada only", "Oceania"], ans: "Sub-Saharan Africa", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "The French variety in Vanuatu is influenced by:", options: ["Bantu languages", "English and local Melanesian languages", "Only French", "Arabic"], ans: "English and local Melanesian languages", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "A shared feature among many postcolonial French varieties is:", options: ["Strict adherence to Académie française rules", "Creative neologisms and semantic extensions", "No creativity", "Decline in usage"], ans: "Creative neologisms and semantic extensions", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "French in Luxembourg is characterized by:", options: ["Trilingual context with Luxembourgish and German", "Monolingual", "Only English influence", "No trilingualism"], ans: "Trilingual context with Luxembourgish and German", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "The term 'franqçais' or 'franglais' is most associated with:", options: ["African varieties", "Canadian varieties with heavy English borrowing", "Belgian", "Swiss"], ans: "Canadian varieties with heavy English borrowing", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" },
{ q: "Overall, the diversity of French varieties today reflects:", options: ["Uniform standardization", "Global spread, colonialism, and local language contact", "Only European evolution", "No diversity"], ans: "Global spread, colonialism, and local language contact", topic: "Varieties of French in the world today" }
